==English==

===Etymology===
Attested since 1576. Possibly imitative of the sound of drinking greedily, or from {{etyl|fro|en}} {{term|gouziller|lang=fro}}, {{term|gosillier||to pass through the throat}}, from {{term|gosier||throat}}, and akin to {{etyl|it|en}} {{term|gozzo||throat; a bird's crop|lang=it}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en-us-guzzle.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|ʌzəl|lang=en}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|guzzl}}

# To [[drink]] (or, sometimes, eat) [[quickly]], [[voraciously]], or to [[excess]]; to [[gulp down]]; to [[swallow]] [[greedily]], [[continually]], or with [[gust]].
#: ''They spent most of their college days '''guzzling''' beer.''
#* '''1720''', [[w:John Gay|John Gay]], “Friday; or, the Dirge” in ''Poems on Several Occasions'', [http://books.google.com/books?id=64UTAAAAQAAJ&pg=PA71&dq=guzzling <small>Google Books</small>]
#*: No more her care shall fill the hollow tray, / To fat the '''guzzling''' hogs with floods of whey.
#* '''1971''', [[w:Leslie Bricusse|Leslie Bricusse]] & [[w:Anthony Newley|Anthony Newley]], “Oompa Loompa, Doompa-Dee-Do”, from ''[[w:Willy Wonka & the Chocolate Factory|Willy Wonka & the Chocolate Factory]]''
#*: What do you get when you '''guzzle''' down sweets, / Eating as much as an elephant eats?
# {{context|intransitive|dated|lang=en}} To consume alcoholic beverages, especially [[frequently]] or [[habitually]].
#* '''1649''', [[w:John Milton|John Milton]], ''[[w:Eikonoklastes|Eikonoklastes]]'', [http://books.google.com/books?id=9Ag1AAAAMAAJ&pg=RA1-PA402&dq=guzzle <small>Google Books</small>]
#*: A comparison more properly bestowed on those that came to '''guzzle''' in his wine cellar.
#* '''1684''', [[w:Wentworth Dillon, 4th Earl of Roscommon|Roscommon]], ''Essay on Translated Verse'', [http://books.google.com/books?id=H0dAAAAAIAAJ&pg=PA198&dq=guzzles <small>Google Books</small>]
#*: Well-seasoned bowls the gossip's spirits raise, Who, while she '''guzzles''', chats the doctor's praise.
#* '''1859''', [[w:William Makepeace Thackeray|William Makepeace Thackeray]], ''The Virginians'', [http://books.google.com/books?id=sx4LAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA311&dq=guzzled <small>Google Books</small>]
#*: Every theatre had it's footman's gallery: […] they '''guzzled''', devoured, debauched, cheated, played cards, bullied visitors for vails: […]
# {{context|by extension|lang=en}} To [[consume]] anything quickly, greedily, or to excess, as if with [[insatiable]] thirst.
#: ''This car just '''guzzles''' petrol.''
#* '''2004''', Mike Rigby, quoted in ''The Freefoam Roofline Report'', [http://michaelrigbyassociates.com/pages/research/quarterly/readreport35166.htm]
#*: China continues full steam ahead and the Americans continue to '''guzzle''' fuel, while supply becomes restricted.

